    {
      "id": "ayc29gu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Types of Weathering"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Weathering is the in-situ disintegration and decomposition of rocks at or near the Earth's surface without transportation.",
        "It represents the initial step in both soil formation and sediment production.",
        "Mechanical weathering physically breaks rocks apart through mechanisms like freeze-thaw, exfoliation, and thermal expansion.",
        "Chemical weathering decomposes rock minerals via oxidation, carbonation, hydrolysis, hydration, and solution.",
        "Factors influencing weathering include climate, rock composition, rock structure, relief, vegetation, and duration of exposure."
      ],
      "id": "mb9tif7"
    },
